          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 32-year-old man with no family history of hypertension has persistent BP of 15</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 32-year-old man with no family history of hypertension has persistent BP of 158/102 mmHg and spontaneous hypokalaemia without diuretic use. Which secondary cause should be investigated first?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pheochromocytoma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pheochromocytoma presents with the classic triad of episodic headache, palpitations, and sweating — spontaneous hypokalaemia is not a feature.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Primary aldosteronism</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Spontaneous hypokalaemia without diuretic use is a hallmark red flag for primary aldosteronism, where excess aldosterone causes sodium retention and potassium wasting.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coarctation of the aorta</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Coarctation is suspected when blood pressure is higher in the arms than the legs. Hypokalaemia is not a feature of this congenital narrowing.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Obstructive sleep apnoea</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>OSA presents with snoring, daytime somnolence, and morning headaches. It does not cause hypokalaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Each secondary cause carries distinct red flags. Spontaneous hypokalaemia without diuretics specifically points to primary aldosteronism — excess aldosterone drives potassium wasting alongside sodium retention and volume expansion."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Spontaneous hypokalaemia in a young hypertensive without diuretic use is a classic pointer to primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome) and warrants an aldosterone-to-renin ratio as the screening test."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
