          "label": "<p>Which investigation in the baseline hypertension workup detects the earliest sig</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which investigation in the baseline hypertension workup detects the earliest sign of renal target organ damage, often before serum creatinine becomes abnormal?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum potassium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Serum potassium screens for primary aldosteronism (a secondary cause of hypertension), not for early renal target organ damage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Microalbuminuria is the earliest marker of hypertensive nephropathy. It indicates glomerular damage and appears well before serum creatinine rises or eGFR falls.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fasting blood glucose</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fasting glucose quantifies overall cardiovascular risk by detecting diabetes, but it does not directly detect renal damage from hypertension.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum creatinine and eGFR</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Creatinine and eGFR assess kidney function but reflect later-stage damage. Urine albumin detects glomerular injury much earlier in the course of hypertensive nephropathy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio detects microalbuminuria — the earliest sign of hypertensive nephropathy. Serum creatinine rises only after significant nephron loss. This is why both tests are in the baseline panel: they detect damage at different stages."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is the earliest renal marker. Remember the key ECG limitation from this section too — Sokolow-Lyon detects only about half of anatomical LVH, so a normal ECG does not exclude cardiac damage."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
