          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 52-year-old patient with type 2 diabetes and proteinuria needs antihypertensiv</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 52-year-old patient with type 2 diabetes and proteinuria needs antihypertensive therapy. Which drug class is the most appropriate first-line choice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thiazide-like diuretic</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Thiazides are first-choice for uncomplicated hypertension but do not provide the renoprotective benefit of RAAS blockade needed when proteinuria is present.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Beta-blocker</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Beta-blockers are reserved for heart failure or post-myocardial infarction. They are not recommended as first-line for uncomplicated hypertension or diabetic nephropathy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ACEi or ARBs are the preferred first-line agents for diabetes with proteinuria. They reduce intraglomerular pressure and slow kidney disease progression beyond their blood pressure lowering effect.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Calcium channel blocker</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CCBs are effective antihypertensives suited for elderly patients and isolated systolic hypertension, but ACEi/ARBs provide superior renoprotection in proteinuric diabetes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "For diabetes with proteinuria, ACE inhibitors or ARBs are the first-line choice because they block the RAAS pathway, reduce proteinuria, and slow nephropathy progression — benefits that go beyond blood pressure reduction alone."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Remember from the narration that ACEi/ARBs are contraindicated in pregnancy — an important caveat. For resistant hypertension on three drugs, spironolactone is the best fourth agent (PATHWAY-2 trial)."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
