          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient on three antihypertensive medications has persistently elevated blood pressure at follow-up. What should be done before adding a fourth drug?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Immediately add spironolactone as the fourth agent</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While spironolactone is the recommended fourth agent for resistant hypertension (PATHWAY-2 trial), confirming the patient is actually taking the existing three drugs must come first.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Assess medication adherence using a non-judgemental approach</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Non-adherence is the commonest cause of apparent treatment failure. Ask: 'Many people find it difficult to remember tablets every day — has that been a challenge for you?' and review dispensing records before escalating.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Switch all three medications to different drug classes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Changing the entire regimen without first confirming adherence wastes time and introduces unnecessary new side effects.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Refer immediately for renal artery imaging</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While secondary causes should be considered in resistant hypertension, confirming adherence is the essential first step — the commonest cause of apparent treatment failure is not taking the tablets.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Before escalating therapy, always check adherence first. Non-adherence is the commonest cause of apparent treatment failure. Use a non-judgemental approach and review dispensing records before adding drugs or investigating further."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The narration models a specific non-judgemental phrasing and recommends reviewing dispensing records — practical skills for every clinical encounter with apparently resistant hypertension."
                }
